nodecode-construct is a rewrite of rtmandreyev's rtmandreyev/construct:

  https://github.com/rtmandreyev/construct
  commit 24a1bc838076c94728376850d461b383d003caa9
  Apache-2.0, Copyright (c) 2026 Artyom Andreyev (LICENSE; the holder is
  named in the repository's NOTICE, the licence text is the Apache License 2.0)

Rewritten: the scene, its format and its provenance. A construct-scene is
still the original's idea -- a named thing as parts, each of a shape from a
closed vocabulary at a real size and a real place, with the facts it is known
by, where each fact came from, and how surely it is known (verified,
user_supplied, traced, inferred, decorative), so a wrong number stays
traceable to what said it. The checks came over with it: every problem
carries its JSON path and the fix to apply, and the scene is compiled once
into triangles and lines that every output is cut from, so a page, a glTF and
an STL of one scene cannot disagree. The exports are the original's too, with
fewer formats: glTF with a node, a mesh and a material per part; OBJ with one
named object per part; a binary STL in millimetres; and the hologram page --
one self-contained HTML file, the lines and faces inlined, turning, each part
named and in its own colour. Every written file is read back and counted
before the cell says it is done, as the original does.

Left out, and why: the original's engine is about 1.3 MB of Python, and a
Nodecode cell carries none of it. So there is no CAD (FreeCAD, STEP, 3MF), no
film renderer (ffmpeg, music, runfilm), no model kit with a booklet, no
headless-browser review images, no NetLogo runs drawn as constructs, and no
joints with animation -- each needs a program or an engine this cell does not
bundle, and each is a build of its own rather than a rewrite of one. The
shipped constructs (sixteen, from a Land Cruiser to the Sainte-Chapelle), the
critic's verdict, the archetypes and the sixteen tutorials are left out as
data: the cell ships two starters, a cube and a shed, and a scene is written
as JSON. The commands `construct_command` reaches (rounds, tracing, lofting,
likeness, terrain, globe, plates) are out for the same reason: each is an
engine, not a verb. What remains is the part of Construct that this image can
do alone, offline, with no Python and no network.
